  "body": "## What happens\n\n`add_tool` gives each new tool the lowest unused number from 1 (camcraft-mcp skill: \"Assigns the lowest unused tool number in 1-99999\"). Many machines keep a fixed pocket for the probe or a tool setter:\n\n- Tormach PathPilot machines: the probe is **T99** by convention.\n- A Hurco VMX30U (WinMax): the export on #2566 has `PROBE` as **T1**.\n- A FANUC Robodrill (31i-A5): the owner's **T1** is the probe.\n\nOn the last two, CamCraft's first cutting tool becomes T1, so a posted program calls the probe for a face or drill operation. Found while making the Robodrill test kit (#2603): its first version faced with T01, and the owner answered \"I will not face with my probe!\". The kit was renumbered to T12-T15 by hand.\n\n## Expected\n\n- A machine file can name reserved tool numbers, for example `\"tooling\": { \"reserved\": [ { \"number\": 1, \"use\": \"probe\" } ] }` (the exact key to be decided).\n- `add_tool` and the tool library skip those numbers for cutting tools; a probe tool takes the probe's number.\n- `set_tool_number` to a reserved number for a tool of the wrong kind is refused by name (or warned), and verify/post warn when a program calls a reserved pocket for cutting.\n- The Tormach machine files reserve T99 (probe); a generic VMC reserves nothing.\n\n## Done means\n\nA test with a machine file that reserves T1 for the probe: the first added end mill gets T2, the probe gets T1, and a program that cuts with T1 is refused by name.\n",
